# {{ .name }}

.NET extractor integration. One run = one sweep: the runner lists the
inbound folder, runs each swept JSON order through the Intropy extractor
pipeline, publishes the result as a CloudEvent to the `{{ .topic }}` topic on
the Dapr pub/sub, deletes the source file, and exits. Scheduling lives
outside the block — the system host locally, a Kubernetes CronJob in
production.

## Build and test

```bash
task build
task test
```

The extractor does not run standalone. It runs via its system host, which
schedules the job (the Aspire dashboard offers "Run now"), provides the
source binding, pub/sub, and platform services, and carries the sample data
used to seed a run. `task test` covers the component-level loop: pipeline
unit tests plus integration tests proving the whole DI graph and the sweep
behave without a sidecar (`task test:unit` / `task test:integration` run the
suites separately). `task coverage` runs the integration suite with
branch coverage (Cobertura report under the integration project's
`TestResults/`).

## Testing strategy

The integration suite runs against `Intropy.Framework.Testing` fakes — no
Dapr sidecar, no Testcontainers, no live platform services. The one principle
throughout: **build the pipeline exactly as production composition does, fake
only the edges, assert on fake state.** NSubstitute appears only where the
testing library deliberately leaves a seam open: the `DaprClient` publish
capture in `PublishIntegrationTests`.

### Layers

| Layer | Project / files | What it pins |
|---|---|---|
| Step unit tests | `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Unit/Process/*Tests.cs` | Each pipeline step's own contract: `Deserializer` (valid JSON, `order_id` stamped into the context), `Validator` (all four branches), `Transformer` (line-content fidelity). (Serialization is the framework's
`CloudEventSerializeStep`, tested in the framework.) |
| Sweep integration | `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Integration/SweepTests.cs` | The full pipeline (deserialize → validate → idempotency → transform → serialize → publish) over one inbound sweep, with `FakeTopic` at the publish edge. Pins the sweep's consumption contract: published, duplicate-cancelled, and incident-routed files are all deleted; technical failures stay and are counted.{{- if .empty }} In this empty-bodies scaffold three tests are not included: business-failure incident routing (which input fails validation is the Validator's to define), duplicate-cancel, and delete-failure-redelivery (the latter two need the duplicate simulation the idempotency extractor lambdas enable). Add them as the corresponding implementation lands.{{- end }} |
| Composition / publish | `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Integration/PublishIntegrationTests.cs`, `CompositionTests.cs` | The real DI graph from `Composition` with only the externals faked. Proves the DI graph builds without a sidecar and that the DI-registered `DaprTopicPublisher` wires pubsub name, topic, and structured-mode CloudEvent encoding correctly (captured via `PublishedMessageCapture`). |

### The edges and their fakes

- **Inbound files** — `InMemoryFileAdapter`. Stateful (List → GetContent →
  Delete behaves consistently), with fault injection: `AddUnreadableFile`
  (per-file read throws), `SetDeleteException` (publish-succeeds-but-delete-
  fails), `ReadException`/`WriteException` (dead source/destination).
- **Topic** — `FakeTopic<Context>`, swapped into DI in place of the production
  `SendStep<Context>` registration (a `DaprTopicPublisher`), exactly like the
  other externals. `Count`/`Events` expose exactly what the real publisher
  would have encoded; `SendException` simulates a dead broker.
- **Platform services** — `FakeIdempotencyServiceClient` and
  `FakeBusinessIncidentServiceClient`. Tests assert on recorded state
  (`Committed`, `Incidents.Single().Data.Description`) directly.
- **DaprClient publish** — the one seam the library doesn't fake. A minimal
  NSubstitute stub forwards `PublishByteEventAsync` into
  `PublishedMessageCapture`; `DecodeCloudEvent()` decodes the structured-mode
  envelope.

### Behaviors pinned

- **Never retry a poison file.** Invalid input routes a business incident and
  consumes the file; a business failure does **not** commit idempotency (a
  corrected redelivery must not cancel as seen-before).
- **Exactly-once survives a delete failure.** Publish succeeds, delete throws
  → next run re-processes, idempotency cancels the duplicate, no double-publish.
- **One bad file never blocks the batch.** Per-file failures (read throws,
  broker down mid-batch) are isolated; remaining files still process, `failed`
  counts only the failed ones.
- **A stuck file fails loudly.** A listed-but-unreadable file is counted and
  kept so the CronJob exits 1, not silently skipped.

### What is deliberately not tested

No Testcontainers or real Dapr sidecar: `PublishIntegrationTests` already
proves the wiring at near-zero cost. Revisit only if the real sidecar's
structured-mode or metadata behavior is ever suspected of diverging.

## Shared contracts

The canonical contract types (`{{ .contract }}`, `OrderLine`) live in the sibling
`../{{ .contracts }}` project, referenced from `src/{{ .name }}.csproj`. It was
scaffolded together with this project (or already existed — components in the
same parent directory share it); the consuming loader deserializes exactly
those records.

## Container image

```bash
docker build --build-context contracts=../{{ .contracts }} -t {{ .appId }} .
```

Multi-stage build on the .NET 10 chiseled runtime. The shared contracts sibling
enters the build as the `contracts` named context (it lies outside this build
context). The image contains only the app — the Dapr sidecar and components
come from the deployment environment.

## Pipeline

Per swept file (`src/Sweep.cs` drives this once per run, hosted by the
framework's `RunToCompletionRunner`, which owns the sidecar lifecycle, tracing,
and the 0/1/2 exit-code mapping — success / job failure / infrastructure
failure — via `src/ExtractJob.cs`):
{{- if .empty }}

1. `Deserializer` parses the JSON into `Source{{ .contract }}`.
2. `Validator` enforces business rules.
3. Idempotency check — cancels duplicates.
4. `Transformer` maps `Source{{ .contract }}` → the shared `{{ .contract }}` contract.
5. `CloudEventSerializeStep` (framework) wraps `{{ .contract }}` in a CloudEvent
   (Subject and Time are mandatory).
6. The Dapr topic publisher sends it to `pubsub` / `{{ .topic }}`.

Every step body throws `NotImplementedException` — this project was scaffolded
with empty bodies for a migration agent to fill in. The idempotency and
business-incident wiring is already in place; point the idempotency extractors
at stable business fields when you implement the steps (see `AGENTS.md`).

The test suite is real and fails out of the box by design: every test drives
the pipeline into a throwing step or extractor lambda (only `CompositionTests`
starts green). The suite flips green piece by piece as the steps are
implemented — the failing tests are the to-do list.
{{- else }}

1. `Deserializer` parses the JSON into `Source{{ .contract }}` and stamps `order_id` into the
   pipeline context.
2. `Validator` enforces required fields and basic business rules; failures are
   routed to the Business Incident Service.
3. Idempotency check — asks the Idempotency Service whether this `OrderId` /
   `OrderDate` combination was already processed; duplicates cancel the run.
4. `Transformer` maps `Source{{ .contract }}` → the canonical `{{ .contract }}` shape from the
   shared contracts project (`../{{ .contracts }}`) that the consuming loader
   deserializes.
5. `CloudEventSerializeStep` (framework) wraps `{{ .contract }}` in a CloudEvent with
   `Subject` = order id and `Time` = order date, validating both at serialize
   time.
6. The Dapr topic publisher sets the envelope source/type and sends it to
   `pubsub` / `{{ .topic }}`.

On success (or an idempotent duplicate) the source file is deleted; a failed
file stays in the inbound folder and is retried on the next scheduled run,
which the run's exit code 1 asks the scheduler to record.
{{- end }}
